### 3. CeraVe

**Country:** United States  
**Website:** https://www.cerave.com/

#### Brand Positioning

CeraVe positions itself around dermatologist-developed skincare and maintaining a healthy skin barrier.

A major part of its identity is its use of three essential ceramides, which are highlighted across its product communication. The brand also places emphasis on dermatologists and science-backed formulations.

Its overall approach focuses on making skincare understandable and accessible rather than presenting it as something complicated.

#### Product Communication

CeraVe explains its products through their ingredients, benefits, and intended uses. It also provides educational content about skincare topics, including the skin barrier and the role of ceramides.

This is useful because customers are given some background about the ingredients and how they relate to their skincare needs, rather than being expected to understand the products on their own.

#### Tone of Voice

- Clear and informative.
- Practical and educational.
- Reassuring and professional.
- Focused on explaining skincare without using unnecessarily complicated language.

#### Visual Identity

CeraVe has a consistent visual identity, particularly its recognisable blue-and-white colour palette.

Its product presentation is relatively simple, with product names, ingredients, and benefits taking priority. This supports its practical and science-focused positioning.

#### Main Customer Appeal

Its communication may appeal to customers looking for straightforward skincare, particularly those interested in hydration, sensitive skin, and skin-barrier care.

#### Key Takeaway for Noura Botanics

CeraVe shows how useful simple product education can be when communicating with customers.

Noura Botanics could apply this to its emails by including short explanations of ingredients and product benefits. For example, an email could introduce one ingredient, explain why it is used, and recommend a relevant product.

This would allow the brand to provide useful information without making its emails too long or overly promotional.

